WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:01.429
OK.

00:00:01.429 --> 00:00:06.310
More and more elements of our country are
slowly but very surely inconsistently shutting

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:07.859
down, at least in blue states.

00:00:07.859 --> 00:00:12.450
I know red states, some some red state governors
are bragging about going out to crowded restaurants

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:13.840
and going to the beach and stuff.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:15.960
But in blue states, we're taking this very
seriously.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.450
And the IRS has now announced that the.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:22.180
Tax deadline for taxes owed will be extended
by 90 days.

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:25.660
Now, I want to be really clear, because some
people are misunderstanding this.

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:30.140
This is the deadline to pay taxes owed not
to file.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:32.680
And it is only federal states may not be.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:34.190
Your state may not be doing this.

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:35.190
All right.

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:40.570
You still need to file your taxes by April
15 or file for the extension, which push pushes

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:42.660
to October fifteen.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:49.750
If you owe money, you get a 90 day extension
on paying the money that you owe.

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:53.010
Then you also need to look at whether your
state is doing the same thing.

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:57.300
California's doing a 60 day extension, so
it's not going to match up exactly with the

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:58.780
federal government.

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:03.750
We've posted a link in the description on
the YouTube video for this story to a document

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:06.060
that is updated for each state.

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:12.490
So Connecticut has extended it until June
15 to pay taxes owed, not to file as far as

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:14.340
we can tell right now.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:18.970
Indiana did delay filing altogether until
June 1, but you've still got to file your

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:19.970
federal.

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:22.799
Massachusetts is waiving penalties for six
months.

00:01:22.799 --> 00:01:25.030
So this is really a big deal.

00:01:25.030 --> 00:01:29.300
The limit for what individuals can delay are
payments of up to a million dollars.

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:33.040
For corporations, it's up to 10 million dollars.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:36.110
There will be no interest in penalties during
the 90 day period.

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:40.631
Now, if you're due a refund, get your taxes
in, because, of course, the sooner you do,

00:01:40.631 --> 00:01:41.870
the sooner you get your money.

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:47.979
I believe that I filed my return on March
9th and I'm told my refund should be coming

00:01:47.979 --> 00:01:49.240
by March 30th.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:50.240
That's 21 days.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:55.150
So it looks like they're on a 20 on a three
week turnaround time on that.

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:59.430
And I you know, but as a business owner, I
estimate my my taxes throughout the year and

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:00.950
make estimated payments.

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:04.810
I know that by overestimating, I'm giving
the government an interest free loan.

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:06.810
It's not drastic, but I prefer to do that.

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:10.209
And no, I'm not going to have to cut a check
at the end of the year.

00:02:10.209 --> 00:02:14.889
And I'm expecting a refund in the next twelve
days or so.

00:02:14.889 --> 00:02:20.889
Important to understand from a macro economic
perspective, all of this stuff that's being

00:02:20.889 --> 00:02:22.849
done is deficit spending.

00:02:22.849 --> 00:02:25.829
Everything that's being discussed is deficit
spending.

00:02:25.829 --> 00:02:30.760
Notice how very quickly we can't afford it
becomes.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:36.859
We can't afford not to with even Republicans
like Mitt Romney talking about what is almost

00:02:36.859 --> 00:02:41.459
at least a single payment of Andrew Yangs
universal basic income of a thousand dollars.

00:02:41.459 --> 00:02:47.890
Now, the counter argument will be, of course,
David, this stuff is temporary versus making

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:51.730
systemic change, which is what Democrats want.

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:56.719
But there's a very simple counter counter
argument, which is, listen, a lot of the things

00:02:56.719 --> 00:03:03.609
we're doing now in an emergency are only necessary
because we didn't do the changes systemically.

00:03:03.609 --> 00:03:11.319
For example, the panic around restaurant workers
who work on tips now not having money, that

00:03:11.319 --> 00:03:17.669
panic would be much smaller if we had a system
in place for paid sick leave, unemployment

00:03:17.669 --> 00:03:20.290
that accounted for tips, et cetera.

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:23.799
We now we're talking about we've got to start
sending cash to people.

00:03:23.799 --> 00:03:27.120
We've got a deficit spend because we have
no such system.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:31.839
And in fact, I tweeted yesterday stuff that
we normally Republicans say we can't afford

00:03:31.839 --> 00:03:33.529
now we can't afford not to.

00:03:33.529 --> 00:03:37.709
And that was exactly the reaction I got from
a lot of people who are thinking short sidedly

00:03:37.709 --> 00:03:39.839
to check out some of these responses.

00:03:39.839 --> 00:03:43.150
Someone said, quote, There is a difference
of supporting people during a crisis where

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:48.189
a lot of people lose their jobs and the normal
situation where you're responsible for your

00:03:48.189 --> 00:03:49.790
own situation.

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:53.919
Another person said to me, temporary relief
is different than long term policy.

00:03:53.919 --> 00:03:55.489
Apples and oranges.

00:03:55.489 --> 00:04:00.400
But the reality is that if you have policies
that strengthen the economy to get people

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:06.969
out of these precarious employment situations
all the time as a baseline, you soften the

00:04:06.969 --> 00:04:12.269
blow of an emergency like this and all the
money you've got to start spending.

00:04:12.269 --> 00:04:17.729
One other example, tweet I got someone said
emergency funding is different than perpetual

00:04:17.729 --> 00:04:18.870
borrowing.

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:23.620
That's very short sighted, guys, because an
ounce of prevention is really worth a pound

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:24.710
of cure.

00:04:24.710 --> 00:04:31.479
If you build in some of these mechanisms all
the time as a baseline, it's much more predictable

00:04:31.479 --> 00:04:36.949
than an emergency where you have to start
haphazardly saying, let's send people a thousand,

00:04:36.949 --> 00:04:43.490
let's send people three thousand five hundred
per kid, bailouts, a payroll tax cut.

00:04:43.490 --> 00:04:49.120
You end up being much more predictable in
your budget when you plan ahead for these

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:50.690
social safety nets.

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:58.129
And you know, anyway, taxes owed being delayed,
restaurants shutting down, schools shutting

00:04:58.129 --> 00:04:59.129
down.

00:04:59.129 --> 00:05:02.360
And we can expect more of this to continue
until things start to settle down.

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:05.139
And we don't know when that will be.

00:05:05.139 --> 00:05:08.729
Understand that right now a lot of this depends
on you and me.

00:05:08.729 --> 00:05:12.280
But also, you know, those people in red states
who are partying at the beach.

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:16.789
There are estimates that cases could peak
in 14 days.

00:05:16.789 --> 00:05:18.199
There are estimates that this.

00:05:18.199 --> 00:05:21.259
Could extend until spring of 2021.

00:05:21.259 --> 00:05:23.009
That's an insane range.

00:05:23.009 --> 00:05:27.690
All that means is we don't have a clue and
it depends on our actions.

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:32.430
Importantly, by the way, I tweeted out yesterday
and I encourage everybody to look at this.

00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:35.110
Sal Khan from Khan Academy.

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:37.189
Brilliant guy, excellent Web site.

00:05:37.189 --> 00:05:44.849
He did an analysis of a blog post which explains
how you have to the number of new cases reported

00:05:44.849 --> 00:05:49.110
is on a significant delay from the number
of actual new cases.

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:55.259
And he is explaining that actual new cases
will likely start to decline long before the

00:05:55.259 --> 00:05:57.419
official numbers reflect it.

00:05:57.419 --> 00:05:59.030
He explains why.

00:05:59.030 --> 00:06:00.030
Really important.

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:03.169
The simple math underpinnings to understand.

00:06:03.169 --> 00:06:04.169
So check that out.

00:06:04.169 --> 00:06:05.379
I tweeted it yesterday.

00:06:05.379 --> 00:06:17.199
I'm D

00:06:17.199 --> 00:06:55.610
Packman

00:06:55.610 --> 00:07:23.229
on Twitter.

